WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.338
When an operation genuinely accepts a
relative path,

2
00:00:03.418 --> 00:00:06.571
first resolve the trusted root to an
absolute lexical path.

3
00:00:06.651 --> 00:00:10.373
Resolve the requested value against that
root to produce a candidate.

4
00:00:10.453 --> 00:00:14.993
Node's path.resolve processes segments
from right to left until it finds an absolute

5
00:00:15.073 --> 00:00:18.605
path, so an absolute request segment can
replace the earlier root.

6
00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:23.163
That behavior is useful because the later
containment check will expose the escape,

7
00:00:23.243 --> 00:00:26.207
but calling resolve alone does not make
the result safe.

8
00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:30.387
Use path.relative to express the candidate
in relation to the resolved root.

9
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:32.548
A result equal to the parent component,

10
00:00:32.628 --> 00:00:36.538
or beginning with a parent component
followed by the platform separator,

11
00:00:36.618 --> 00:00:37.817
represents an escape.

12
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:41.428
An unexpectedly absolute result also
indicates different roots,

13
00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.337
such as different Windows drives.

14
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:46.129
An empty result means candidate and root
are equal.

15
00:00:46.209 --> 00:00:49.300
Whether equality is acceptable depends on
the operation;

16
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.848
listing a root may be intentional, while
deleting it should fail.

17
00:00:52.928 --> 00:00:55.830
This comparison is clearer than a naive
string prefix.

18
00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.324
A directory named files and a sibling
named files-backup share characters but not a

19
00:01:00.404 --> 00:01:01.918
containment relationship.

20
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:05.341
Appending a separator to prefix checks can
reduce one mistake,

21
00:01:05.421 --> 00:01:09.962
yet path.relative communicates path
components and platform separators directly.

22
00:01:10.042 --> 00:01:13.321
Use Node's path implementation for the
deployment platform,

23
00:01:13.401 --> 00:01:17.500
and do not invent universal lowercasing
for case-insensitive filesystems.

24
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.166
Windows drive letters, UNC paths,
backslashes,

25
00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:24.913
and volume-specific case behavior require
tests in the actual supported environment.

26
00:01:24.993 --> 00:01:28.210
A reusable helper can make lexical
containment consistent,

27
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.182
provided its contract is explicit:

28
00:01:30.262 --> 00:01:34.046
root and candidate are resolved in the
same intended platform context;

29
00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:36.901
parent escapes and absolute relative
results fail;

30
00:01:36.981 --> 00:01:38.873
root equality is a caller decision.

31
00:01:38.953 --> 00:01:41.854
The helper verifies syntax after lexical
resolution.

32
00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:46.223
It does not follow symbolic links or prove
that the requester may access the file.

33
00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:50.339
Callers still need object authorization,
operation-specific rules, and,

34
00:01:50.419 --> 00:01:53.510
where the threat model includes filesystem
indirection,

35
00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.860
canonical-path or safer-open reasoning.
